The code might throw an error on Windows because the process.stdin and process.stdout streams in Node.js behave differently on Windows and Unix-based systems. On Windows, process.stdin is in "raw" mode by default, which means that it does not emit the data event until a newline character is entered.

In the code, you are piping stdin to stdout before registering the data event listener on stdin. This means that the data event may not have been emitted by the time the stdin.pipe(stdout) method is called. On Unix-based systems, this code may still work as expected because process.stdin is typically not in "raw" mode by default.

To fix this issue on Windows, you can put the stdin.pipe(stdout) method inside the data event listener for stdin like this:

const stdin = process.stdin;
const stdout = process.stdout;

stdin.on("data", (msg) => {
  console.log("terminal input", msg);
  stdout.write(msg.toString().toUpperCase());
});

// Don't need to call stdin.pipe(stdout) here

@pro7on there's a difference between a web stream and a node stream. Web streams don't have `pipe` method, but both web streams and node streams are available in Node 17+.  You have two options (see below)

// OPTION 1: Convert web stream into a Node stream, then pipe
import {Readable} from 'node:stream'
const response = fetch(url)
Readable.fromWeb(response.body).pipe(process.stdout)

// OPTION 2: Work with web streams
const response = fetch(url)
for await (const chunk of response.body)
  process.stdout.write(chunk)

